  "notes": [
    "This diocese's cycle is DAILY, not weekly -- every calendar day of the month has its own entry, unlike every other diocese in this corpus so far. Only September and October 2026 are transcribed here because those are the only two months the diocese had published/linked as of this ingest (per the roster's own 'September-October 2026' coverage note); November and December entries do not yet exist as a source to transcribe from.",
    "Most daily entries name one or more individual clergy by name (e.g. 'The Rev. Albert Moser') rather than a parish or a generic category. These are recorded as `category` subjects, the same convention already used elsewhere in this corpus for a named person or office with no parish/place of their own (e.g. Alaska's 'Presiding Bishop Rowe'). When an entry combines a parish AND a named clergy person (e.g. 'St. Andrew's Church, Scotia -- The Rev. Dr. Dean Kellerhouse'), both are recorded as separate subjects in the same entry -- the source's own dash construction is not asserted to mean the named person is that parish's rector, only that both are named together on that date.",
    "Two apparent typos in the source are preserved verbatim, not corrected: 'Chris the King' (September 5, almost certainly 'Christ the King') and 'Sactuary Task Force' (October 21, almost certainly 'Sanctuary').",
    "The September PDF's printed weekday names match the real 2026 calendar exactly (checked: September 1, 2026 is in fact a Tuesday, as printed). The October PDF's printed weekday names do NOT match the real 2026 calendar -- they are internally consistent with each other (a regular repeating 7-day sequence) but shifted one day early throughout (e.g. October 1, 2026 is printed as 'Wednesday' but is actually a Thursday). The day-of-month numbers themselves are used as given for this file's `date` fields regardless, since this schema stores ISO dates, not weekday labels; the weekday-label error is disclosed here for the record, not treated as a reason to alter the dates."
  ],
